    "case_id": "638",
    "case_summary": "A 54-year-old woman living in upstate New York with a medical history significant for idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia (ICL) originally diagnosed in 2015 with an annual CD4 cell count ranging between 14 and 18 cells/µL. She initially presented to her primary care physician with a sore throat. When symptoms did not improve after one week, she was referred to an otolaryngologist. The sore throat persisted despite a trial of omeprazole and fluctuated in intensity over the following months. Several months later, the patient began to experience dysphonia along with odynophagia. Throughout this course, the patient had no systemic symptoms (fever, weight loss, myalgias) or pulmonary symptoms.\n\nLaryngoscopy revealed thickening of the epiglottic mucosa with irregularity and white plaques. A repeat laryngoscopy demonstrated progression of the plaques.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  * Laryngeal cultures initially grew Streptococcus mitis, Streptococcus salivarius, Rothia mucilaginosus, Neisseria flavescens/subflava, and Candida albicans\n  * Urine histoplasma antigen: positive at 0.3 ng/mL\n  * Acid-fast stain and culture: negative\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  * Computed tomography of the chest revealed subtle ground-glass opacities in the left upper lobe\n\n- Pathological Examination:\n  * Laryngeal biopsy revealed rare hyphae and yeast forms on Grocott methenamine silver stain with no evidence of malignancy or granulomas\n  * Fungal culture grew Histoplasma capsulatum, identified by typical tuberculate microconidia\n\n- Images:\n  * Figure 1 Title: Laryngoscopy findings\n  * Figure 1 Description: Patient's epiglottis (A) and larynx (B) showing white plaques and thickening of the mucosa\n  * Figure 2 Title: Microscopy findings\n  * Figure 2 Description: (A) Mold with cottony texture and white-tan reverse after 16 days incubation, (B) Lactophenol cotton blue tease mount preparation showing large, thick-walled, round tuberculate macroconidia at 400× magnification",
